1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,726
What stops two clicks on the pay button from charging the card twice?

2
00:00:03,726 --> 00:00:09,297
Two requests to confirm the same payment can arrive at the same moment, and both would try to debit.

3
00:00:09,297 --> 00:00:15,071
One conditional update moves the payment from confirmed to debiting, and only one caller can win it.

4
00:00:15,071 --> 00:00:19,226
Anyone who loses the claim simply gets the payment as it stands.

5
00:00:19,226 --> 00:00:25,761
The winner fixes the route: the website's live terminal for the card's scheme, with its MID and TID.

6
00:00:25,761 --> 00:00:32,392
It fixes the fee too: the most specific commission rule, the fee and the merchant's net, kept on the payment.

7
00:00:32,392 --> 00:00:38,380
With no terminal or no fee rule, the payment fails right there, before any money moves.

8
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:45,785
Every call to the scheme is written to an operation journal first, and the database allows only one debit per payment.

9
00:00:45,785 --> 00:00:54,202
Then the card module asks the scheme through its adapter, with our payment id as the reference, so a retry is never paid twice.

10
00:00:54,202 --> 00:00:59,095
An approval makes the payment paid, with the scheme's reference number, the RRN.

11
00:00:59,095 --> 00:01:02,154
A decline is final, with our own code for why.

12
00:01:02,154 --> 00:01:07,238
No answer leaves it unknown, never declined, because the money may already have moved.

13
00:01:07,238 --> 00:01:11,619
Every move goes through the status table, so a paid payment can never go back.

14
00:01:11,619 --> 00:01:17,440
The buyer sees the result on the page, and the bank sees the fee, the terminal and the RRN.

15
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:20,976
Next, seeing inside the system when something is slow.

